---

<Layout
  title="About | Mentor AI"
  description="Vision, mission, impact, and team behind Mentor AI. University of Michigan research on AI-mediated feedback."
>
  <Header />
  <main
    id="main-content"
    role="main"
    class="bg-background flex-1 pt-12 pb-24 md:pt-20 md:pb-40"
  >
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-none px-6 md:px-8 lg:max-w-6xl">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl py-24 text-center sm:py-32 lg:py-48">
        <ScrollReveal>
          <h1
            class="text-umich-blue text-5xl leading-none font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-7xl"
          >
            Enabling Timely, High-Quality Feedback at Scale
          </h1>
        </ScrollReveal>

        <ScrollReveal delay={0.1}>
          <p
            class="mt-8 text-lg font-medium text-pretty text-gray-700 sm:text-xl sm:leading-8"
          >
            We envision a future where every student receives timely,
            high-quality feedback and guidance during and after they practice,
            without increasing the burden on instructors.
          </p>
        </ScrollReveal>
      </div>
<!--
      <div class="mx-auto mt-20 max-w-3xl lg:mx-0">
        <ScrollReveal delay={0.15}>
          <h2
            class="text-umich-blue text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl"
          >
            Why feedback is hard to scale
          </h2>
        </ScrollReveal>
        <ScrollReveal delay={0.2}>
          <p class="mt-6 text-lg/8 text-gray-700">
            Formative feedback supports learning in open-ended writing, but
            producing it well takes sustained attention from instructors and
            teaching assistants, especially in large courses.
          </p>
        </ScrollReveal>
        <ScrollReveal delay={0.25}>
          <p class="mt-4 text-lg/8 text-gray-700">
            Automated comments can be fast but risky: they can misread
            discipline-specific reasoning or drift from your rubric, and they
            may not provide the kind of guidance students can use to revise.
            What we need is not automation alone, but <strong
              class="font-medium text-gray-900">human judgment</strong
            > steering <strong class="font-medium text-gray-900"
              >AI assistance</strong
            >.
          </p>
        </ScrollReveal>
      </div> 
      <div class="mx-auto mt-20 max-w-3xl lg:mx-0">
        <ScrollReveal delay={0.3}>
          <h2
            class="text-umich-blue text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl"
          >
            Our Mission
          </h2>
        </ScrollReveal>
        <ScrollReveal delay={0.3}>
          <p class="mt-6 text-lg/8">
            Providing detailed, personalized feedback is challenging in large
            writing-intensive courses. <strong class="font-medium text-gray-900">FeedbackWriter</strong> — our deployed and peer-reviewed tool for TAs — has been evaluated in a randomized controlled trial with 354 undergraduate students. AI-mediated feedback through FeedbackWriter
            led to significantly higher-quality revisions than human-only TA
            feedback (Cohen's d = 0.50); course post-test scores did not differ significantly
            between conditions in that study. Our mission is to translate these
            findings into practical tools that help instructors and TAs deliver
            consistent, rubric-aligned feedback, without claiming learning gains
            beyond what the evidence supports.
          </p>
        </ScrollReveal>
      </div>
